     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height:normal;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\">We have been working\r\nwith some spatial disease models with a set of penalized priors, however we\r\nnotice something unusual in other words we would not expect to see. Find the\r\ncode down below. <u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:normal;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\">Basically for model “mod3”\r\nthe mean precision for group is way higher than the posterior mean of random\r\neffects when we use &quot;pc.prec&quot;, param = c(0.01, .01))? If we penalize\r\nup to .7 seems to be reasonable why?<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:normal;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\"><br></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:normal;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\">thank you very much</span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:normal;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\"><br></span></p><p class=\"Mso